<article class="env-proposition" id="0553">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0553">Proposition <span data-tag="0553">3.5.7.16</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Kan complex and let $n$ be an integer. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan complex $X$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists an $n$-truncated Kan complex $Y$ which is homotopy equivalent to $X$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists an $(n+1)$-coskeletal Kan complex $Y$ which is homotopy equivalent to $X$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(4)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The tautological map $X \rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{n+1}(X)$ is a homotopy equivalence. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication $(2) \Rightarrow (1)$ follows from Corollary <a data-tag="0550" href="/tag/0550">3.5.7.8</a>, the implication $(3) \Rightarrow (2)$ from Example <a data-tag="054Q" href="/tag/054Q">3.5.7.2</a>, and the implication $(4) \Rightarrow (3)$ from the observation that $\operatorname{cosk}_{n+1}(X)$ is a Kan complex (Proposition <a data-tag="052N" href="/tag/052N">3.5.3.23</a>). We will complete the proof by showing that $(1)$ implies $(4)$. Assume that $X$ is $n$-truncated; we wish to show that the tautological map $u: X \rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{n+1}(X)$ is a homotopy equivalence. Since $\operatorname{cosk}_{n+1}(X)$ is also $n$-truncated (Example <a data-tag="054Q" href="/tag/054Q">3.5.7.2</a>), it will suffice to show that $u$ is $(n+1)$-connective (Corollary <a data-tag="0551" href="/tag/0551">3.5.7.14</a>). This is a special case of Remark <a data-tag="052M" href="/tag/052M">3.5.3.22</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
